# Tokenization
A cute teddy bear is reading.
How to cut this sentence to pass to a model? -> Tokenization
- Arbitrary
    eg. A, cute, teddy bear, is, reading, .
- word
    eg.A, cute, teddy, bear, is, reading, .
    con: Similar words might be tokenized like reading & read
- subword
    eg. A, cute, ted, ##dy, bear, is, read, ##ing, .
- character 
   eg. a, c, u, t, e, t, e, d
   con: longer sequnce, challenge to understand what each token mean

## Tokenization Methods: Pros and Cons

| Method | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **Word-level** | • Simple<br>• Interpretable | • Risk of OOV (out-of-vocabulary)<br>• Does not leverage knowledge of root |
| **Subword-level**<br>e.g. WordPiece, BPE | • Leverages common prefixes and suffixes<br>• Learned from the data | • Risk of OOV, though less than word-level |
| **Character-level** | • Small chance of OOV<br>• Robust to casing and misspellings | • Makes computations slower<br>• Embeddings not interpretable |

