# Dependnecy on Neural networks and softmax  read

Word2vec is a tiny neural network. Two ideas are needed first.

## Neural network (the minimum needed)

- A **layer** is a matrix multiply: `output = input × W` (W = weights, learned).
- A network is layers stacked: `input → layer 1 → layer 2 → output`.
- **Training** = guess, measure how wrong the guess was (the **loss**), nudge W to be less wrong, repeat over lots of data.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    X["input<br>(one-hot token)"] --> W1["layer 1<br>weights W1"] --> H["hidden vector<br>(the embedding)"] --> W2["layer 2<br>weights W2"] --> S["scores<br>one per vocab word"] --> P["softmax<br>probabilities"]
```

Why this matters for word2vec: **the weights of layer 1 *are* the embeddings.**
A one-hot vector times W1 just selects one row of W1, so each row is one token's embedding (a lookup table).
The network is never used for its predictions. It is trained so that W1 becomes useful.

## Neural network: a worked example

**Task:** predict whether a student **passes** (1) or **fails** (0) from hours studied and hours slept.
This student studied **4 h**, slept **2 h**, and actually **passed** (true answer = 1).

> **Note on the numbers:** every *starting* weight below (0.5, -0.3, 0.2, 0.8 in W1 and 0.6, 0.4 in W2) is **made up** by me, chosen to be small and easy to calculate by hand. They are not derived from anything. In a real network they start as small **random** numbers, and training moves them to useful values. Everything *after* the starting weights (hidden values, score, probability, loss, gradients, new weights) is calculated from them.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    X1["studied = 4"] --> A["hidden neuron A"]
    X1 --> B["hidden neuron B"]
    X2["slept = 2"] --> A
    X2 --> B
    A --> O["output score"]
    B --> O
    O --> P["sigmoid → P(pass)"]
```

### The terms

| Term | Meaning | In this example |
|---|---|---|
| **Input `x`** | The numbers fed in | `x = [4, 2]` (studied, slept) |
| **Weight** | A knob: how much one connection matters. Learned, not typed in | e.g. studied→A = 0.5 (starting value **made up** for the example; random in a real network) |
| **W1** | The **matrix of all weights in layer 1** (inputs → hidden neurons). One row per input, one column per hidden neuron | see below (starting values made up) |
| **W2** | The matrix of all weights in layer 2 (hidden neurons → output) | `[0.6, 0.4]` (starting values made up) |
| **Hidden neuron** | A unit that weighs its inputs and adds them up. "Hidden" because you never see its value outside the network | A and B |
| **Hidden vector `h`** | The list of all hidden neuron values. In word2vec, this is the embedding | `[2.4, 0.4]` |
| **Activation function** | A small squashing step after the sum. Without it, stacked layers collapse into one | ReLU: negatives become 0 |
| **Score** (logit) | The raw output number, before turning it into a probability | 1.6 |
| **Sigmoid** | Squashes any score into 0 to 1. It is softmax for 2 classes | 1.6 → 0.83 |
| **Loss** | How wrong the guess was (`-log` of the probability given to the true answer) | 0.18 |
| **Gradient** | Per weight: "if I increase you a little, does the loss go up or down, and by how much?" Found by backpropagation | W2: `[-0.40, -0.07]` |
| **Learning rate** | How big each nudge is. You choose it | 0.1 |

### Activation functions: ReLU and Sigmoid

An **activation function** is a small step applied to a neuron's number after the multiply-and-add. Two common ones:

**ReLU: "ignore negatives"**

```
ReLU(x) = x    if x is positive
        = 0    if x is negative
```

| input | output |
|---|---|
| 2.4 | 2.4 |
| 0.4 | 0.4 |
| -1.5 | **0** |

- Works like a one-way gate: positive signals pass unchanged, negative ones are blocked.
- In the example both hidden values were positive, so ReLU changed nothing. If neuron B had been -0.5, it would become 0 and stay silent for this student.
- **Why use it:** without an activation, stacked layers collapse into one big multiplication, so extra layers add nothing. The bend at zero lets the network learn curved patterns. It is also very cheap to compute, so it is the usual choice for **hidden layers**.

**Sigmoid: "squash into 0 to 1"**

Takes any number and squashes it into a value between 0 and 1:

| input (score) | output |
|---|---|
| -5 | 0.007 (almost 0) |
| 0 | **0.5** (coin flip) |
| 1.6 | **0.83** |
| 5 | 0.993 (almost 1) |

- The output can be read as a **probability**, so it is used on the **output layer** for yes/no questions. In the example, score 1.6 becomes 0.83, meaning "83% pass".
- It is the two-class version of softmax (below). Softmax does the same job for many classes, such as every word in a vocabulary.

| | ReLU | Sigmoid |
|---|---|---|
| Output range | 0 to infinity | 0 to 1 |
| Job | Blocks negatives, keeps positives | Turns a score into a probability |
| Typical place | Hidden layers | Output layer (2 classes) |
| In the example | `[2.4, 0.4]` unchanged | `1.6 → 0.83` |

### The weights

Layer 1 as a matrix (rows = inputs, columns = hidden neurons). These starting numbers are **made up**, not calculated:

```
            neuron A   neuron B
W1 =  studied [ 0.5      -0.3 ]
      slept   [ 0.2       0.8 ]
```

### Layer 1 and Layer 2 at a glance

Every number on one picture, with the computation on each connection:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph IN["Input"]
        X1["studied = 4"]
        X2["slept = 2"]
    end
    subgraph L1["Layer 1 (W1) + ReLU"]
        A["Neuron A<br>4×0.5 + 2×0.2 = 2.4<br>ReLU → 2.4"]
        B["Neuron B<br>4×(-0.3) + 2×0.8 = 0.4<br>ReLU → 0.4"]
    end
    subgraph L2["Layer 2 (W2) + Sigmoid"]
        S["Score<br>2.4×0.6 + 0.4×0.4 = 1.6<br>Sigmoid → 0.83"]
    end
    X1 -- "×0.5" --> A
    X1 -- "×(-0.3)" --> B
    X2 -- "×0.2" --> A
    X2 -- "×0.8" --> B
    A -- "×0.6" --> S
    B -- "×0.4" --> S
    S --> P["P(pass) = 0.83"]
```

The same thing as a table:

| | Layer 1 (input → hidden) | Layer 2 (hidden → output) |
|---|---|---|
| **Weights** | W1 (2×2): `[[0.5, -0.3], [0.2, 0.8]]` | W2 (2×1): `[0.6, 0.4]` |
| **Takes in** | `x = [4, 2]` | `h = [2.4, 0.4]` |
| **Multiply and add** | A: `4×0.5 + 2×0.2 = 2.4`<br>B: `4×(-0.3) + 2×0.8 = 0.4` | `2.4×0.6 + 0.4×0.4 = 1.6` |
| **Activation** | ReLU: `[2.4, 0.4]` (both positive, unchanged) | Sigmoid: `1.6 → 0.83` |
| **Gives out** | `h = [2.4, 0.4]` | `P(pass) = 0.83` |

Read it left to right: the output of Layer 1 is the input of Layer 2. Each layer does the same two things: **multiply and add with its weights, then apply its activation.**

### Step 1: forward pass (make a guess)

`h_raw = x × W1`, the multiply-and-add for every hidden neuron at once:

- A: `4×0.5 + 2×0.2 = 2.4`
- B: `4×(-0.3) + 2×0.8 = 0.4`

ReLU keeps both (they are positive), so `h = [2.4, 0.4]`.

`score = h × W2 = 2.4×0.6 + 0.4×0.4 = 1.6`

(The 0.6 and 0.4 here are the made-up starting weights of W2. Everything else in these lines is calculated from them.)

`P(pass) = sigmoid(1.6) = 0.83`. The network says **83% pass**.

### Step 2: loss

True answer is "pass", so the ideal probability is 1.
`loss = -log(0.83) = 0.18` (small, because the guess was close).

### Step 3: backpropagation (who is to blame?)

Work backwards from the loss to get a gradient for every weight.

| Weight | Gradient | Reading |
|---|---|---|
| A→output (W2) | -0.40 | raising it lowers the loss a lot |
| B→output (W2) | -0.07 | raising it helps a little |
| studied→A (W1) | -0.40 | raising it helps |
| slept→A (W1) | -0.20 | raising it helps |
| studied→B (W1) | -0.27 | raising it helps |
| slept→B (W1) | -0.13 | raising it helps |

Neuron A's weights get more blame because A was more active (2.4 vs 0.4), so it had more influence on the answer.

### Step 4: gradient descent (nudge)

`new weight = old weight - learning rate × gradient`

The "Before" column is the made-up starting weight. The learning rate 0.1 is also **chosen by us** (a hyperparameter). The "After" column is calculated, e.g. `0.64 = 0.6 - 0.1 × (-0.40)`.

| Weight | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| A→output | 0.60 | **0.64** |
| B→output | 0.40 | **0.41** |
| studied→A | 0.50 | **0.54** |
| slept→A | 0.20 | **0.22** |

### Step 5: check it helped

Same student, new weights: P(pass) goes **0.83 → 0.87**, loss goes **0.18 → 0.14**.
Repeat over thousands of students and the weights settle on values that predict well.

### How this maps to word2vec

| | This example | Word2vec |
|---|---|---|
| Input `x` | hours studied, slept | one-hot word |
| W1 | inputs → 2 hidden neurons | vocab size → embedding size (e.g. 300) |
| Hidden vector `h` | `[2.4, 0.4]` | the word's embedding |
| W2 | hidden → 1 score | embedding → one score per vocab word |
| Output | sigmoid, P(pass) | softmax, P(each word) |
| Weights we keep | all | **W1 only** |

## Softmax

Layer 2 outputs raw **scores** (any number, positive or negative), one per word in the vocabulary.
Softmax turns scores into **probabilities** (all between 0 and 1, summing to 1):

$$softmax(z_i) = \frac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_j e^{z_j}}$$

- `e^z` makes every score positive and exaggerates the gap between big and small scores.
- Dividing by the total makes them sum to 1.

### Note: what is `e` (Euler's number)?

`e` is a **fixed number, about 2.718**, like π is about 3.14. It is not a variable. `e^z` means "2.718 raised to the power z". In code: `np.exp(z)`.

| z | e^z |
|---|---|
| -2 | 0.14 |
| -1 | 0.37 |
| 0 | **1** |
| 1 | 2.72 |
| 1.6 | 4.95 |
| 2 | 7.39 |

Why sigmoid and softmax use it:

1. **Never negative.** Even `e^-5` is a small positive number (0.007). Probabilities can't be negative, and `e^z` guarantees that for any score `z`.
2. **Keeps the order.** A bigger score always gives a bigger `e^z`, so the highest score stays the most likely.
3. **Widens gaps.** Scores 2.0 and 1.0 are only 1 apart, but `e^z` turns them into 7.39 and 2.72, so the leader pulls ahead.
4. **Smooth.** No jumps in the curve, which makes the backpropagation nudges work well.

Another base (2 or 10) would also work. `e` is the standard choice because the gradient maths comes out cleanest with it.

Where it comes from (not needed for the course): `e` appears in continuous growth, like compound interest applied constantly instead of once a year.

Also, in the softmax formula `z` is the raw score (also called a **logit**) that a layer outputs before it becomes a probability. `z_i` is the score of one word, and the sum runs over all the scores.

Worked example (3-word vocabulary):

| word | score z | e^z | probability |
|---|---|---|---|
| teddy bear | 2.0 | 7.39 | 7.39 / 11.21 = **0.66** |
| book | 1.0 | 2.72 | 2.72 / 11.21 = **0.24** |
| soft | 0.1 | 1.11 | 1.11 / 11.21 = **0.10** |
| **total** | | 11.21 | **1.00** |

## Loss: how wrong was the guess?

**Cross-entropy**: `loss = -log(probability given to the correct word)`

- Correct word got 0.66 → loss = -log(0.66) = **0.42** (small, good)
- Correct word got 0.10 → loss = -log(0.10) = **2.30** (big, bad)

Training nudges W1 and W2 to push that probability up. Words that appear in similar contexts get pushed the same way, so their rows in W1 end up close together. That is how "teddy bear" and "soft" become neighbours.

### Note: entropy and cross-entropy

**Entropy: how unpredictable is something, on average?**

Start with **surprise**: an unlikely event surprises you a lot, a likely one barely does.

```
surprise = -log(probability)
```

| event | probability | surprise (bits) |
|---|---|---|
| the sun rises | 0.99 | ~0.01 (not surprising) |
| fair coin lands heads | 0.5 | 1 |
| a 1-in-10 event happens | 0.1 | 3.32 (quite surprising) |

**Entropy = the average surprise** over all outcomes, weighted by how often each happens:

```
entropy = - sum( p × log(p) )
```

| situation | probabilities | entropy (bits) |
|---|---|---|
| Fair coin | 0.5, 0.5 | **1.0** (max uncertainty for 2 outcomes) |
| Biased coin | 0.9, 0.1 | **0.47** (mostly predictable) |
| Always heads | 1.0, 0 | **0** (no uncertainty) |

**Cross-entropy: how surprised are you when your beliefs are wrong?**

Entropy assumes you know the true probabilities. Cross-entropy measures the average surprise when you predict with the **model's** probabilities `q` while reality follows the **true** ones `p`:

```
cross-entropy = - sum( true p × log(model q) )
```

- Model matches reality: cross-entropy is as low as it can be (equal to the entropy).
- Model is off: cross-entropy is higher. The extra is the price of the wrong beliefs.

Example: a fair coin (true `[0.5, 0.5]`) but the model believes `[0.9, 0.1]`:
`0.5×0.15 + 0.5×3.32 = 1.74` bits, versus entropy 1.0. The extra 0.74 bits is what training tries to shrink.

**The special case used above (classification).** The truth is **one-hot**: the correct word has probability 1, all others 0. Every term in the sum vanishes except one:

```
cross-entropy = -log(probability the model gave to the correct word)
```

That is exactly the loss formula at the top of this section.

| | teddy bear | book | soft |
|---|---|---|---|
| True | **1** | 0 | 0 |
| Model | 0.66 | 0.24 | 0.10 |

`loss = -ln(0.66) = 0.42`. (The true distribution has entropy 0, so the whole 0.42 is the model's error.)

Why it works as a loss:

- Correct answer gets probability near 1: loss near 0.
- Correct answer gets probability near 0: loss shoots up (`-ln(0.01) = 4.6`), so confident wrong answers are punished hard.
- It is smooth, so backpropagation can compute good nudges.

**Bits vs nats.** Bits use `log₂`; libraries and the numbers in these notes (0.42, 2.30) use the natural log `ln` (nats). Same idea, different scale.

**One line:** entropy is the average surprise of the truth; cross-entropy is the average surprise when you use the model's guesses instead. It is the classification loss, and training pushes it down.

## Cost to remember

Softmax sums over the **whole vocabulary** for every training example. With 100k+ words that is slow, which is why real word2vec uses shortcuts (negative sampling, hierarchical softmax).

## The proxy tasks

Word2vec trains the network on one of two made-up tasks, just to get good embeddings:

1. CBOW (continuous bag of words)
   - Predict the middle word from the surrounding context words.
   - eg. context: `A`, `cute`, `is`, `reading` -> target: `teddy bear`

   ```mermaid
   flowchart LR
       A["A"] --> T["teddy bear"]
       B["cute"] --> T
       C["is"] --> T
       D["reading"] --> T
   ```

2. Skip-gram
   - Opposite of CBOW: predict the surrounding context words from the middle word.
   - eg. input: `teddy bear` -> targets: `A`, `cute`, `is`, `reading`

   ```mermaid
   flowchart LR
       T["teddy bear"] --> A["A"]
       T --> B["cute"]
       T --> C["is"]
       T --> D["reading"]
   ```


## Termination condition
- Tracking loss    

## Putting it together

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Raw text<br>(billions of words)"] --> B["Make (input, target) pairs<br>with CBOW or skip-gram"]
    B --> C["One-hot input × W1<br>= embedding"]
    C --> D["× W2 → scores → softmax"]
    D --> E["Cross-entropy loss<br>vs the true word"]
    E --> F["Nudge W1, W2"]
    F --> B
```

1. Slide a window over the text to create pairs (no human labels needed, the text labels itself).
2. Predict the target word, measure the loss, update the weights.
3. After training, **throw away layer 2 and keep W1**: one learned vector per token.

Limitation (leads to Transformers): one vector per word, regardless of context. "bank" (river) and "bank" (money) get the same embedding.



